Real server permission proof
I created no real session and made no live model call.
I used five GET requests on port 38307. No POST or DELETE reached that server.
The first agent-by-id request returned 404. Preliminary directory queries selected the server default.
I discarded those results and used the native deep-object query location[directory].
The scoped GET /api/agent returned the exact synthetic non-git job location.
The build permission list ended with eight matching deny rules.
The normalized shell deny represents the legacy bash deny.
The scoped GET /api/config returned the job's opencode.json and the same eight normalized denies.
The job's git rev-parse check exited 128, so the proof does not depend on a git repository.
Confidence is high for project discovery and effective build permissions in a fresh non-git job.
I did not verify a real session's agent selection.
I printed permission projections only, without credential values or system prompts.

